{
  "dom_id": "step-q334183",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}=\\frac{-12}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x+5)}^{4}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 0=0\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
